    $astroMig_master$> Twice now our account of the solar system's origin has failed in the same way. In L4 we met hot Jupiters — giants orbiting other stars at a twentieth of Mercury's distance, far too hot and sparse to have built them. In L5 we met the Mars problem — simulations that overpredict Mars's mass five- to tenfold, implying its zone was stripped of material. Both failures trace to a single assumption, so natural we made it without stating it: **that planets remain where they form.** This chapter abandons it, and in doing so turns the solar system from a static arrangement into something with a violent, contingent history. We will establish that a planet embedded in a gas disk **exchanges angular momentum with it and migrates**; introduce **mean-motion resonance**, which turns weak tugs into decisive ones; and then reach the centrepiece — the **Nice model**, in which Jupiter and Saturn crossing the 2:1 resonance flung Uranus and Neptune outward, scattered a disk of icy debris into the Kuiper Belt and Oort Cloud, and rearranged the outer solar system. **Our solar system is not where it was built.** And we will close Unit I with an honest reckoning: the very phenomenon the Nice model was built to explain — the Late Heavy Bombardment — is now itself in doubt.

## The suspicious ages of the Moon rocks

Between 1969 and 1972, twelve people walked on the Moon and brought home **382 kilograms of rock** — still the only material we possess from another world collected by hand, from known locations, with geological context. Much of it is **impact melt rock**: rock melted by a large impact and resolidified, which resets the radiometric clock, so dating it tells you *when the impact happened.* Researchers dated these samples expecting to map lunar bombardment across four and a half billion years. What they found was strange.

**The ages clustered.** Rather than spreading evenly, a striking proportion of the impact-melt ages fell in a narrow window around **3.8 to 4.0 billion years ago** — sample after sample, from different sites, pointing to the same era. The natural reading was dramatic: the Moon, and the whole inner solar system, suffered a **spike** of bombardment around 3.9 billion years ago. It was named the **Late Heavy Bombardment**.

{{image: Apollo 17 | An astronaut collecting samples on the Moon during Apollo 17, the last crewed lunar landing. The 382 kilograms of rock returned by Apollo remain the only geologically-documented material we have from another world — and dating the impact-melt rocks among them revealed something strange: their ages cluster around 3.8–4.0 billion years, suggesting a sudden spike of bombardment more than half a billion years after the solar system formed. Explaining that spike drove one of the boldest ideas in planetary science — and, as it turns out, the samples themselves may be misleading us.}}

Here is the puzzle. The solar system formed 4.568 billion years ago, so the spike came roughly **670 million years later** — long after the system had settled. And that is very hard to explain, because the leftover debris of accretion should be swept up *early*: planets clear their neighbourhoods quickly, and the impact rate should decline smoothly from the start, like a fire burning down. A sudden *surge* half a billion years on requires that a large population of small bodies was **stored somewhere stable and then suddenly released.** What could reach into a quiet, mature solar system and hurl a swarm of debris at the inner planets? The answer, proposed in 2005, is that **the giant planets moved** — not gently, but in a sudden system-wide instability. There is a twist I will develop honestly at the end: the Late Heavy Bombardment that motivated all this is now itself under serious challenge. But first: how does a planet move at all?

## How planets move: the physics of migration

A planet orbiting alone stays put forever — that is Kepler and Newton, exact for two bodies. But a young planet is **embedded in a massive gas disk**, and that changes everything. The planet's gravity raises **spiral density waves** in the disk — overdense wakes trailing and leading it — and those wakes have mass and pull back on it. The two sides pull opposite ways: material *interior* to the orbit runs ahead (it orbits faster) and pulls the planet **forward**, pushing it outward; material *exterior* trails behind and pulls it **backward**, dragging it inward. If these balanced, nothing would happen. They do not — in most disk models the outer torque wins, giving net **inward migration.** This is the central idea: **a planet embedded in a gas disk exchanges angular momentum with it, and the exchange is generally unbalanced, so the planet migrates.**

For low-mass planets (below ~10–20 Earth masses), this is **Type I migration**, and its striking feature is *speed*: inward timescales of order $10^4$ to $10^5$ years for an Earth-mass planet — against a disk lifetime of millions.

```example
migration-speed
```

That is a genuine problem: taken at face value, Type I migration should sweep growing embryos into the star before the disk clears, predicting that terrestrial planets shouldn't survive. Proposed rescues include *planet traps* (at pressure bumps the torque can reverse, stalling migration — note the link to L5's dust traps), thermal torques, and late assembly after the gas is gone. Its rate and even direction are still debated — another place where the standard picture, taken literally, destroys the planets it should build.

Once a planet exceeds roughly a Jupiter mass, it clears an annular **gap** in the disk (the process from the end of core accretion, and the likely origin of some ALMA rings). With a gap open, it is no longer subject to unbalanced local torques; instead it is **locked to the disk's own viscous drainage** and carried inward with it, like a log in a slow river. This is **Type II migration**, on the disk's viscous timescale of $10^5$ to $10^6$ years — and **it is the accepted explanation for hot Jupiters.** A giant forms beyond the snow line, as L4 requires; it opens a gap; and it is carried inward, potentially from 5 AU to 0.05 AU. The theory of *where* giants form was never wrong — 51 Pegasi b did form beyond its snow line. It simply did not stay there. **Migration is not exotic; it is the default behaviour of a planet in a gas disk.** The question is not whether our giants migrated, but how much, in which direction, and when. What makes migration produce a *catastrophe* rather than a gentle drift needs one more idea.

## Resonance: how small tugs become large effects

Gravitational tugs between planets are usually negligible — that was the point of the Sun's mass dominance. Jupiter tugs on Saturn, but over one orbit the tug points mostly one way, then another, and the effects **cancel** — *unless the tugs repeat in phase.* Two bodies are in a **mean-motion resonance** when their orbital periods form a ratio of small integers: a 2:1 resonance means the inner body completes exactly two orbits for each one of the outer's. When that happens the geometry of their encounters **repeats** — they return to the same relative configuration, at the same orbital points, over and over — so each gravitational tug is applied in the same direction at the same phase, again and again. **Instead of averaging out, the perturbations accumulate.** It is the principle of pushing a child on a swing: feeble pushes, timed to each cycle, build a large amplitude. Watch where successive conjunctions fall as you tune the period ratio:

```resonance
```

Resonances are everywhere once you look — the **Kirkwood gaps** swept empty in the asteroid belt, **Pluto** locked in 3:2 with Neptune (which is what keeps it safe despite crossing Neptune's orbit), the **Galilean moons** in a 4:2:1 chain that powers Io's volcanism, gaps in **Saturn's rings**. They are the mechanism by which weak interactions become structurally decisive, giving the solar system sharp features rather than smoothness.

```example
resonance-orbit
```

Now combine the two ideas. If planets migrate, their periods change; if their periods change, the *ratio* between two planets changes — so **migrating planets inevitably sweep across resonances.** Ordinarily two planets sit at some non-resonant ratio and their tugs average away. But a migrating pair passes *through* resonance, and while inside it their perturbations add coherently, pumping eccentricities dramatically in a short time. **Migration guarantees resonance crossings, and resonance crossings can destabilize an entire planetary system.** That is the mechanism at the heart of the Nice model.

## The Nice model: a solar system rearranged

In 2005, researchers in Nice, France proposed that the outer solar system we see is **not the one that formed** — that the giants began in a substantially different configuration and were violently rearranged. It has become the standard framework for the outer system's history. Scrub through it:

```nicemodel
```

The model begins with the giants **compact** — all within about 15 AU, Jupiter near 5.45, Saturn near 8.2, the ice giants around 11–14 — beyond a **massive disk of leftover planetesimals**, some **35 Earth masses** of icy debris out to ~35 AU (exactly the residue L5's slow outer-system accretion would leave). Then a slow process: the giants scatter planetesimals, and each scattering exchanges angular momentum. Neptune, Uranus, and Saturn tend to scatter objects inward, gaining angular momentum and drifting slowly **outward**; Jupiter, massive enough to eject bodies from the system entirely, drifts slightly **inward.** This **divergent migration** proceeds gently over hundreds of millions of years — until Jupiter and Saturn's period ratio reaches **2:1.**

And there the catastrophe unfolds. The two most massive planets, now exchanging coherent tugs, have their **eccentricities pumped up**, and they destabilize everything else. **Uranus and Neptune are flung outward** onto wild orbits, plunging into the planetesimal disk — and in many versions Neptune, which may have formed *inside* Uranus, is thrown clean past it, so the two **swap places.** Neptune ploughing through 35 Earth masses of debris **scatters it comprehensively**: outward into the **Kuiper Belt**, to enormous distances forming the **Oort Cloud**, out of the system entirely, and *inward*, toward the terrestrial planets — the flux that was the original explanation for the Late Heavy Bombardment. As the disk is consumed, the interactions damp the giants' eccentricities and they settle into roughly today's orbits.

{{image: Pluto | Pluto, imaged by New Horizons in 2015. Pluto is locked in a 3:2 mean-motion resonance with Neptune — orbiting the Sun exactly twice for every three Neptune orbits — which is what protects it, guaranteeing the two are never close even though Pluto's orbit crosses Neptune's. Such resonant populations are a direct fingerprint of the Nice model: as Neptune migrated outward, its resonances swept through the debris disk and captured objects into them. Pluto did not form where it orbits; it was shepherded there by a migrating Neptune.}}

The model's appeal is the breadth of what falls out of one event. It explains: **the Late Heavy Bombardment's timing** (its original motivation); **the giants' present orbits**, including their modest eccentricities and inclinations, hard to produce if they formed in place; **the Kuiper Belt's resonant populations**, especially the plutinos captured as Neptune's resonances swept outward — a specific, checkable prediction; **the Jupiter Trojans**, captured during the chaos; **the irregular satellites** of the giants, best explained as captured bodies; and **the depleted asteroid belt**, holding less than 5% of the Moon's mass. **One event, six independent explanations** — which is why the model was adopted so fast, and why it survives even as its original motivation weakens.

```example
period-ratio
```

## The Grand Tack: solving the Mars problem

The Nice model addresses the outer system, hundreds of millions of years on. But L5 left a problem in the *inner* system, requiring a different and much earlier episode. Restated: simulations from a smooth disk make a planet at Mars's location five to ten times too massive, but Mars is only **0.107 Earth masses** — so its zone near 1.5 AU must have been strongly **depleted**, the disk truncated near 1 AU. What could truncate it? The **Grand Tack** (2011) says: Jupiter did, by paying the inner solar system a visit while the gas was still present. Jupiter forms first near 3.5 AU and, as a gap-opening giant, migrates **inward** to about **1.5 AU**, ploughing through and scattering the inner disk. Saturn forms later and migrates inward faster, catches Jupiter, and the two lock in a **3:2 resonance** — whereupon, sharing a common gap, the torque balance reverses and the pair **tacks back outward together** (the name is from sailing: running one way, then sharply reversing course), until the gas disperses and leaves Jupiter near 5.4 AU.

It explains **the Mars problem directly**: Jupiter's inward excursion removes material beyond about 1 AU, so when the terrestrial planets later assemble, Earth and Venus form from a well-stocked region while Mars forms at the *edge* of a truncated disk, starved — and simulations including it reproduce Mars's actual mass. It also explains **the asteroid belt's low mass and mixed composition**: Jupiter sweeps most of the belt away, and on its passage scatters bodies from *beyond* the snow line inward, **implanting** the wet C-types among the dry S-types — which refines L4 (the compositional gradient is real, but the outer-belt population may be immigrants rather than natives). And it may have **delivered water to Earth**, a question for later. But the Grand Tack is **elegant and influential, yet contested**: it needs a specific sequence of migration rates and resonance capture, and alternatives exist (the disk may simply have been depleted beyond 1 AU from the start). Hold it more loosely than the snow line — a leading hypothesis, not an established fact.

## Is the Late Heavy Bombardment real? A case study

Now the honest reckoning, and the most instructive part of the chapter. **The Late Heavy Bombardment — the impact spike the Nice model was built to explain — has come under serious challenge.** The case *for* it: Apollo impact melts cluster at 3.8–4.0 Gyr, and a smooth decline would spread ages across lunar history. But the case *against* has accumulated, and one objection is serious. **All six Apollo landing sites lie on the lunar nearside, and most are within reach of ejecta from the Imbrium basin**, one of the largest impact structures on the Moon. If many of the dated "impact melts" are actually Imbrium ejecta, then they all record *one event* — and a single colossal impact would masquerade as a solar-system-wide spike. The apparent cluster could be a **selection artefact of where the astronauts happened to land.**

{{image: Mare Imbrium | Mare Imbrium, the vast dark basin filling much of the Moon's northwest — the scar of one of the largest impacts in lunar history. Most Apollo landing sites lie close enough to be blanketed by its ejecta, which raises an unsettling possibility: many of the "impact melt" rocks that seemed to record a solar-system-wide bombardment 3.9 billion years ago may in fact all date this single event. A dataset can be perfectly accurate and still profoundly misleading if it is not representative.}}

Other analyses of the crater record are consistent with a **smoothly declining** bombardment rather than a spike, and evidence from ancient zircons, lunar meteorites (which sample the Moon more randomly), and asteroid samples does not cleanly confirm a sharp cataclysm. **The current position is genuinely unsettled** — most would agree the evidence for a sharp cataclysm is much weaker than it appeared in 2005. So does the Nice model fall with the LHB? **No — and the reason is instructive.** The model was *motivated* by the LHB but is *supported* by six lines of evidence, five of which — the giants' orbits, the Kuiper Belt's resonant populations, the Trojans, the irregular satellites, the depleted belt — are **entirely independent of the lunar record.** What changes is the **timing**: the original model needed a *late* instability (500–700 Myr) precisely to match a 3.9 Gyr bombardment; without that constraint, modellers now favour an **early** instability, within ~100 Myr, which other evidence prefers. **The Nice model has been decoupled from the Late Heavy Bombardment** — retained for what else it explains, re-dated as the evidence that fixed it late has weakened.

The lesson is clean, and students rarely see it stated: **the relationship between a theory and the observation that inspired it is not permanent.** A model invented to explain X may turn out to explain A through F, and if X later dissolves, it survives on A through F, suitably modified. And a subtler point about evidence: the Apollo ages *looked* like strong, direct data — real rocks, radiometrically dated — but **evidence is only as good as its sampling.** Six sites clustered on one hemisphere, possibly dominated by one basin's ejecta, may not represent the Moon. **A dataset can be entirely accurate and still profoundly misleading if it is not representative** — a warning that reaches far beyond planetary science. It should also calibrate how you hold this chapter: migration is **certain**; a Nice-style instability **very likely**; its precise timing **actively revised**; the Grand Tack **plausible and contested**; the LHB as a sharp cataclysm **doubtful.** Five confidence levels, and a good education keeps them distinct.

## Unit I closes: the solar system as a history

Gather this chapter, and then the whole unit. Planets migrate because they exchange angular momentum with the gas disk they are born in — fast enough (Type I) that the puzzle is why terrestrial planets survive, and inevitably enough (Type II) to carry giants inward and make hot Jupiters. Migration changes period ratios, so migrating planets sweep through **resonances**, where tugs repeat in phase and accumulate like pushes on a swing. The **Nice model** applies this to our system: compact giants beyond a 35 Earth-mass debris disk, drifting apart until Jupiter and Saturn cross the 2:1 resonance, whereupon eccentricities spike, the ice giants are flung outward (Neptune past Uranus), and the disk is scattered into the Kuiper Belt, the Oort Cloud, and inward — one event explaining six things. The **Grand Tack** closes L5's Mars problem: Jupiter migrating in to 1.5 AU and back out, truncating the inner disk. And the **LHB itself is now doubtful** — the Apollo samples may reflect one basin, not the Moon — yet the Nice model survives on its independent support, its timing revised. **A theory can outlive the observation that inspired it.**

And with that, Unit I is complete. The six chapters form one argument. **L1** established the yardstick — the AU, hunted across three centuries — and the thesis: *distance is destiny.* **L2** examined the Sun: 99.87% of the mass, organizing the system, imposing an inverse-square flux gradient from 668% of Earth's light at Mercury to 0.11% at Neptune. **L3** told the origin: a cold cloud collapsing and, because angular momentum is conserved, necessarily flattening into a disk — explaining the flatness, the common direction, and the near-circular orbits all at once. **L4** found the boundary: the disk's temperature put water's condensation at ~2.7 AU, and because water is the most abundant condensable substance, crossing that line multiplied solids two- to fourfold — with fossils still visible in the planets' densities, the asteroid belt, and the Galilean moons. **L5** built the worlds: dust to pebbles securely, the unresolved metre-size barrier, then runaway growth, giant impacts, and the core-accretion race the giants won. **L6** set them moving.

Here is the unit's central claim, fully assembled. **The solar system is one family, born from one cloud, sorted by one variable — and then rearranged by its own dynamics.** Its architecture needs two kinds of explanation, and telling them apart is the skill this unit exists to teach. Some features are **necessary**: the flatness (angular momentum), the rocky/giant division (the snow line and the race against dispersal), the compositional gradient. Run the experiment again and you would get them again — which is why they generalize to the thousands of exoplanet systems now known. Other features are **contingent**: the particular masses of the inner planets (stochastic giant impacts), the exact positions of Uranus and Neptune (which resonance was crossed, and when), whether Mars is small (whether Jupiter tacked). Run it again and these would differ. **A complete account of a planetary system needs both — physics and history.** To treat it as a *causal history* rather than a catalogue is precisely to ask of every feature whether it had to be so, or merely happened to be so. **Next, Unit II opens with comparative planetology** — the framework that turns Mercury, Venus, Earth, the Moon, and Mars from five separate subjects into five controlled experiments in the same physics, and lets us finally ask why Venus roasts, why Mars died, and why Earth alone remained habitable.$astroMig_master$,
